1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,731
How can one lake hold about a

2
00:00:01,731 --> 00:00:03,879
fifth of all the fresh surface water

3
00:00:03,879 --> 00:00:04,416
on Earth?

4
00:00:04,766 --> 00:00:07,575
This is Lake Baikal, in Siberia, Russia.

5
00:00:07,575 --> 00:00:09,752
From above it looks like a long

6
00:00:09,752 --> 00:00:13,193
blue crescent, about six hundred kilometers long.

7
00:00:13,543 --> 00:00:15,794
It's not the biggest lake by area.

8
00:00:15,794 --> 00:00:17,713
But it is by far the deepest,

9
00:00:17,713 --> 00:00:20,295
more than sixteen hundred meters at its

10
00:00:20,295 --> 00:00:23,142
deepest point. You could stack three Empire

11
00:00:23,142 --> 00:00:25,525
State Buildings on top of each other

12
00:00:25,525 --> 00:00:27,644
and still not reach the surface.

13
00:00:27,994 --> 00:00:30,770
All that depth means an enormous amount

14
00:00:30,770 --> 00:00:32,976
of water. More than all five of

15
00:00:32,976 --> 00:00:35,610
North America's Great Lakes combined.

16
00:00:35,960 --> 00:00:38,118
Baikal is also the oldest lake in

17
00:00:38,118 --> 00:00:40,930
the world, around twenty five million years

18
00:00:40,930 --> 00:00:42,826
old. It sits in a crack where

19
00:00:42,826 --> 00:00:45,573
the Earth's crust is slowly pulling apart,

20
00:00:45,573 --> 00:00:47,992
so it keeps getting wider and deeper.

21
00:00:48,342 --> 00:00:50,783
Being so old and isolated, it has

22
00:00:50,783 --> 00:00:53,963
animals found nowhere else, like the Baikal

23
00:00:53,963 --> 00:00:56,181
seal, one of the only seals in

24
00:00:56,181 --> 00:00:58,992
the world that lives entirely in fresh

25
00:00:58,992 --> 00:00:59,435
water.

26
00:00:59,785 --> 00:01:02,405
In winter, the surface freezes so clear

27
00:01:02,405 --> 00:01:04,823
and thick that cars drive across it,

28
00:01:04,823 --> 00:01:07,174
and you can see cracks running deep

29
00:01:07,174 --> 00:01:08,383
into the blue ice.

30
00:01:08,733 --> 00:01:11,285
The water is incredibly clear. In some

31
00:01:11,285 --> 00:01:13,300
places you can see up to forty

32
00:01:13,300 --> 00:01:16,592
meters down, thanks to tiny shrimp-like creatures

33
00:01:16,592 --> 00:01:18,674
that filter and clean the lake.

34
00:01:19,024 --> 00:01:21,774
More than three hundred rivers flow into

35
00:01:21,774 --> 00:01:24,181
Baikal, but only one flows out, the

36
00:01:24,181 --> 00:01:26,862
Angara River. It would take hundreds of

37
00:01:26,862 --> 00:01:29,200
years to replace all of its water.

38
00:01:29,550 --> 00:01:32,407
Around Baikal live the Buryat people, who

39
00:01:32,407 --> 00:01:35,403
consider the lake sacred. Olkhon Island, in

40
00:01:35,403 --> 00:01:37,493
the middle of the lake, is one

41
00:01:37,493 --> 00:01:40,281
of the most spiritual places in Siberia.

42
00:01:40,631 --> 00:01:43,639
And because the rift underneath keeps pulling

43
00:01:43,639 --> 00:01:46,447
apart, scientists think Baikal may one day

44
00:01:46,447 --> 00:01:49,121
become a brand new ocean, splitting Asia

45
00:01:49,121 --> 00:01:51,528
in two. But that would take millions

46
00:01:51,528 --> 00:01:52,129
of years.

47
00:01:52,479 --> 00:01:54,421
Would you dare to drive across a

48
00:01:54,421 --> 00:01:56,484
frozen lake more than a mile deep?

49
00:01:56,484 --> 00:01:57,941
Tell me in the comments.

